        <body>
            <div xml:id="d383">

                <pb facs="0518" n="438" sameAs="frus:frus1901/pg438"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Thieriot to Mr.
                            Hay.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Lisbon</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1901-01-14"><hi rendition="#italic">January 14,
                        1901.</hi></date></dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 62.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In accordance with the Department’s
                    instructions Nos. 29 and 30, and accompanying copies of letters from the Ayer
                    Company, I have had a special interview with the minister of foreign affairs,
                    with the view of prevailing upon him by the most forcible representations to use
                    his best influence to obtain from the board of health authorization for the
                    importation and sale of at least the two medicines of most importance to the
                    Ayer Company, and his excellency has promised me that he will do all in his
                    power to bring the board of health to this decision.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">J. H. Thieriot</hi>,<lb/><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Chargé d’Affaires.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
